outline: the studio's goal, built in Go. One tree from the root, read once
and drawn twice: on the terminal, and as a page in the browser. Taken:
studio, the frame, hosts fm and ed as they are
(`todo/studio/outline:/The frame: studio hosts/`); it is the one new
piece, for a frame fits none of the others
(`design/studio:/Built on sys, not beside it/`).

The shape, as built: pieces that pass messages, not one program
  fm, ed     as they were, each a tangle tree, drawing through sys/outline,
             and on the terminal through sys/view
  studio     the frame, a tangle tree too: 1-frame 2-term 3-page 4-lens
             5-verbs 6-main
  packages   outline, tty, view, watch and state, and figure, the one
             primitive built so far

The structure is the tree, not a language
  Folders and files carry it down to the idea, and inside a file the
  note's own outline carries the rest: its indent, items and labels. A
  file per idea, not per field: field files cost a file per label, make a
  task slower to write, and change nothing on the page. An s-expression is
  only a way to print the tree for a program, as `medium/page/list` --json
  does; no page language sits between the tree and a view.

A primitive is a kind the views know: the text it matches, such as !290,
or a whole note, as a figure is; how the terminal draws it, how the page
draws it, and what can be done with it. It is a package of its own, as
sys/figure is, and a view asks it by kind: no plugin system and no
runtime library, as a page already carries its parts
(`todo/studio/shelf:/carries its parts by/`). A primitive earns its place
when the notes keep needing what the reader cannot say, and it replaces a
program, or two views need it.

The terminal is one view, behind an abstraction
  fm does what a file manager does, and ed what an editor does; how either
  is shown is abstracted, in sys/outline. A program says what to show as
  nodes, the same Node as the outline: fm's rows, ed's lines and
  selections. It takes keys by name. A view draws the nodes and hands back
  the names:

    type View interface {
        Draw(n *Node, status string, state []string) // the tree, the status
        Key() (Key, bool)                            // what was pressed, where
        Ask(prompt, def string) (string, bool)       // a line typed in answer
        Size() (w, h int)                            // how much shows at once
        Copy(text string)                            // text to the clipboard
    }

  Any system that implements View can show and work fm and ed: the
  terminal on tty, a page, gio's window. Nodes, not cells: cells would make
  the page a terminal in a browser, as ttyd does, where nodes let it draw a
  pill or a chip. Interaction is the other half of a view: a key comes back
  by name, with the node it was said on, as a click names one, and the text a
  paste brings; a line typed at a prompt comes back through Ask. The window
  is the view's own, so the terminal scrolls by itself and a page would
  scroll natively. fm and ed draw, take their keys, ask and copy through
  View, their screens as they were. Still on tty directly: fm's title and the
  suspend around a spawned program or an fzf picker, and ed's check for keys
  still waiting. The keys . puts back to replay a change wait in ed's own
  queue, ahead of the view's. A page view still runs fm or ed here, as a
  process beside the files: the page is its screen and its keys, over
  localhost with a token. That is the step studio puts off
  (`todo/studio/shelf:/It makes the page an app/`), and this goal takes it.

Messages, not calls
  A foundation of all of sys, as the outline is:
  `README.md:/everything talks in messages/`. A program and its view pass
  only plain data: in one process a call whose arguments are data; down a
  pipe or a socket a JSON line each, as outline.Stream writes them. fm -w
  and ed -w run the program with its view in another process, gio's
  window (`gio/README`), which outline.Window starts. It draws ed's
  text as text, in the terminal's font, and hands ed every key.
    to the view      draw: the tree, the program's word and its state;
                     ask: a prompt; copy: text for the clipboard
    to the program   key: its name, the node it was said on, the rune a
                     click fell on, and any text; answer: the line
                     typed; size: how much the view shows
  A message joins when a view needs one, as a primitive does. ed sends
  every line, until a measure says lines must be read on demand. What only
  a terminal can do stays with it: in a window fm says so of a picker,
  help, or a program run in its place; o and e open beside it, and run.

The frame: studio hosts the pieces
  studio is the frame, a tangle tree of its own; fm and ed stay as they
  are. The frame is a note, `studio/frame`: its lines stack down, the
  lines under one go across, and so on, turn about. Each line is a slot,
  a pane or a split into slots; a share such as 40% is its part of its
  parent, and the rest of the line is what runs in it.
  Each slot is a process that speaks messages, so composing is the design
  and not an accident of where it runs:
    fm -s, ed -s  their view at the other end of stdin and stdout, the
                  same JSON lines fm -w sends its window
    ed -f         the thing at cur as written, and editable: source is
                  ed, chasing cur; its lines are nodes named path:line
    lens          a slot a frame may add: the views derived from the
                  thing, diff, figure and run, read-only
    shell         the one slot that is a terminal: tmux in control mode
                  runs it, and studio draws its screen in the slot
  studio draws each slot's nodes in its region, keeps which slot has the
  eye, and hands it the keys; a click goes to the slot under it, as a key
  on the node there. The same note lays out on a page, each slot the
  page's view of the same process, and in gio. tmux is not the frame: it
  runs the shell, and it is where studio was started, if it was.

The socket: studio's words are tmux's, for slots
  Programmable as tmux is: studio listens on a socket. list, the slots and
  what runs in each; send, keys or text into one; read, its nodes, not a
  scrape of its screen; focus; lens; and watch, a line for each thing that
  happens, a draw, the eye moving, cur, as tmux -C streams its
  notifications. studio send shell make is one line from any program, an
  agent's too. Until the socket, send, read and lens are studio's verbs,
  which tmux carries out.

The look, as the samples drew it, built
  fm's and the lens's top line says where the cursor is, a path from home
  in its parts, a line by its label, and its keys in chips; under a rule,
  its layouts, folder and thumbs, the one in use lit, or in a lens slot
  the lenses as tabs, each with its number, the one shown lit with its
  tally, a lens not built dim. What the cursor is on is lit across its
  whole row, with a blue edge; numbers are quiet and the scaffolding
  faint, so that names are the loudest thing, and a thing's facts, its
  size and age or a link's target, stand at the right when they fit
  whole. As thumbs a folder is cards, each a name, its kind and its
  facts. Beside fm ed shows the thing as written, a line a row after its
  number, the bottom line its status and nothing on top: an editor.
  The palette is the page's tokens in their dark form
  (`tty/palette.go:/The palette/`), in 24-bit colour, on the terminal's
  own black, as the shell beside them is (`tty/palette.go:/Ground is/`).

Who holds what
  program  the data, the cursor and the selection: what its verbs act on.
           fm sends the folder it stands in: its rows, and the cursor's.
  view     how it shows, in one of two layouts, t turning to the other: the
           folder as a list, or as thumbs, cards read left to right. It moves
           the cursor among what it shows, down, up, to the last, a page, and
           in the terminal to a row by its number; as thumbs right and left
           move too, and down and up go a row of cards. It tells the program
           where the cursor landed. Going in and out is the program's, for it
           changes what is shown, and so is g, the first file, which skips
           the folder's own . and .. rows that only fm knows.
  between  ideas, by name: down, in, rename. A key is one way to say one
           and a click another. A view does the ideas of moving itself, and
           hands the rest to the program with the node they were said on. ed
           passes its keys through: an editor's keys are its language.

The status line, at the bottom only, in every view
  On the left the program's word, what just happened or what it asks; on
  the right the state, a few short words from whoever holds each: the
  program's, [2] selected, cut:3, mark:x, dotfiles, the search; then the
  view's, its layout and where the cursor is, 12/34. Short of room the
  word is cut, for the state says what holds now. The top line keeps
  where: the path, or an item's address. A prompt takes the bottom line
  while it asks. ed says its mode, + while unsaved, and held while that
  keeps it from cur's file; its views add where the cursor is, line:col.

fm and ed as primitives, three ways
  taken     studio hosts fm and ed, each a process as it is, its view
            at the other end of messages
            (`todo/studio/outline:/The frame: studio hosts/`). fm does
            not grow to hold the others, as was proposed. ed stays ed:
            studio never edits text itself, and e hands ed the item, in
            the terminal or on the page.
  later     fm and ed as Go packages, each a component with its keys and
            its views, their mains ten lines, so one process can host
            both on one page. A primitive is a Go interface from the
            start, so this stays open.
  not       both stitched into one main.go: each has its own main, the
            two share the top-level names binding and userHome, and
            tangle walks with WalkDir, which does not follow a linked
            folder.

The check: `outline/text_test.go` carries one note through every rule,
and over every text file in sys the nodes say, word for word and in
order, what the file says.

What retires once it holds: page/todo and page/list, the python that reads
and draws notes (`design/studio:/A piece that serves/`).

